  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since we are given the lengths of the opposite side and the hypotenuse, we can use sine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{6}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $x$ using inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =(27.486\\,4…)^{∘} \\\\ x & ≈{27.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
